{
  // KARVAN plan 2 of 2 - two client sites, or two pages of one site, at the same time.
  // Spec: docs/history/CONDUCTOR-KARVAN.md (Part II). Research: docs/dev/NEXT-ERA-FINDINGS-2026-08-04.md.
  //
  // AUTHORED, NOT LAUNCH-READY. Two things must happen first:
  //  1. Karvan core completes - in particular K3.1. A lane worktree contains NO .conductor/ at all
  //     (the state gitignore is a bare star), so a lane cannot hold state until the catalogue lives
  //     outside the tree. This is a hard dependency, not a preference.
  //  2. K7.1 re-measures the token budget with `conductor budget`. The limits block below carries the
  //     core plan's numbers; correct them from that measurement before launching this one.
  //
  // Same authoring traps as the core plan: `plan set` strips these comments; {model} must stay in both
  // args and resumeArgs; templatesDir is relative to THIS FILE'S directory while tracker and planDoc
  // are repo-relative.
  "version": "1.0",
  "planVersion": 1,
  "name": "Karvan lanes - the caravan splits",
  "repo": "C:/code/conductor",
  "satelliteRepos": [],
  "tracker": "plans/karvan/LANES-TRACKER.md",
  "planDoc": "docs/history/CONDUCTOR-KARVAN.md",
  "branchPattern": "^feat/karvan-lanes$",
  "pauseOnBlocked": true,
  "agent": {
    "command": "claude",
    "args": [
      "-p",
      "{prompt}",
      "--output-format",
      "stream-json",
      "--verbose",
      "--dangerously-skip-permissions",
      "--model",
      "{model}",
      "--session-id",
      "{sessionId}"
    ],
    "resumeArgs": [
      "-p",
      "{prompt}",
      "--output-format",
      "stream-json",
      "--verbose",
      "--dangerously-skip-permissions",
      "--model",
      "{model}",
      "--resume",
      "{claudeSessionId}"
    ],
    "output": "stream-json",
    "model": "claude-opus-5"
  },
  "advisor": {
    "enabled": true,
    "command": "claude",
    "args": [
      "-p",
      "{prompt}",
      "--output-format",
      "json",
      "--model",
      "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001"
    ],
    "output": "json",
    "timeoutMinutes": 6
  },
  "setup": {
    "command": "dotnet build-server shutdown; exit 0",
    "timeoutMinutes": 2
  },
  "teardown": {
    "command": "dotnet build-server shutdown; exit 0",
    "timeoutMinutes": 2
  },
  "progress": {
    "kind": "markdown-table"
  },
  "stages": [
    {
      "id": "L1",
      "title": "Git safety is code, not prose",
      "sessions": 3,
      "ownerGate": false,
      "kind": "deliver",
      "notes": "SPEC: docs/history/CONDUCTOR-KARVAN.md section L1. Nothing parallel should exist above an engine that force-deletes unmerged branches, and today it does. L1.1 makes clean-tree and branch discipline enforceable rather than advisory - the branch-pattern warning in the run loop is currently the only enforcement and a session on the wrong branch is told so and proceeds; there is no requireCleanTree anywhere in the tree. Strict is the default for new plans, lenient stays available for existing ones. L1.2 adds push and force-push guards and a per-checkpoint commit-convention check. L1.3 IS DONE - it landed early, in karvansara-edge KS4.4, per that plan's ND-8, and this stage rescopes to the multi-lane generalization. The fix was the one this stage called its most important: the mutating lane runner force-deleted the scratch branch in its finally block, so the moment a merge went red or lost a race to another lane, a full session of gate-green work was force-deleted with only the reflog holding it. What shipped: Git.DeleteBranchSafe - branch -d, with no force overload anywhere in the tree and a test that fails the build if branch -D reappears in src/; WorktreeDrop, which deletes the directory itself - children first, the tree's own .git link LAST so a blocked drop stays a worktree git can still see, read-only cleared, bounded retry - and then prunes, instead of the worktree removal that swallowed the exact IO exception a locked Windows build output produces; a kept branch reported by name in the log and in the result; WorktreeSweeper.LiveCount for counting live trees against a concurrency limit; conductor worktree [--reap] as the verb that lists and reaps; and an orphan sweep at engine startup that only ever touches trees carrying conductor's own prefix and never one whose sidecar marker names a live process. AttemptWorktree carries the single-lane base of the lifecycle: cut at the base commit, a clean attempt diff into the evidence taxonomy, ff-only merge on green, drop-whole on failure. WHAT REMAINS is the multi-lane generalization, plus the one piece KS4.4 could not reach and deliberately did not fake: running a SESSION inside a worktree needs the state-dir redirect, because PlanConfig.StateDir is derived from Repo and swapping the repo path moves state.json, the logs, the evidence and the engine lock with it. That is L3.1's job, not this stage's."
    },
    {
      "id": "L2",
      "title": "Repos are first class",
      "sessions": 4,
      "ownerGate": false,
      "kind": "deliver",
      "notes": "SPEC: docs/history/CONDUCTOR-KARVAN.md section L2. The owner's unit of parallel work is a repository or a worktree - two client sites, or two pages of one site - so repos come before same-repo stage parallelism. Today satellite repos are a bare list of strings doing exactly two things: the verdict diffs them for commits, and doctor checks they resolve. They do not branch, checkout, gate, commit, push or assert cleanliness, and everything else is prose in promptExtra - the owner's own nine-repo CI plan carried eight numbered traps doing by hand what this stage moves into the engine. L2.1 promotes them to first-class entries with an id, a path, a role, a branch pattern and gates, keeping the old key as a back-compat alias so every existing plan keeps working, and makes doctor report each one. L2.2 adds per-repo branch and clean-tree assertions and per-repo gates. L2.3 adds coordinated commit and push plus per-repo verdict attribution, and turns the anchor-commit rule into an engine-side expectation instead of a paragraph in a template. L2.4 shows every repo in the Face with its branch, dirty state, ahead-behind and the commits this session landed there."
    },
    {
      "id": "L3",
      "title": "A lane is a real session",
      "sessions": 4,
      "ownerGate": false,
      "kind": "deliver",
      "notes": "SPEC: docs/history/CONDUCTOR-KARVAN.md section L3. This is the unglamorous stage that makes the scheduler possible, and it is where the real distance to parallel work lives. A lane today spawns the agent through the process runner directly: no session runner, and therefore no cost rows, no token budget or soft break, no rollover handling, no resume rail, no stall watchdog, no verdict pass, no tracker claim, no MCP task tools and no transcript - success is inferred from whether the merge happened. L3.1 gives a lane a state redirect to the run's canonical store and makes one writer own it; the store opens with write-ahead logging and NO busy timeout, which is exactly the configuration that starts failing under a second writer, so set the timeout and prove it under contention. L3.2 makes a lane run a real session with everything in that list. L3.3 adds per-lane environment - a lane index, a base port plus offset, its own state dir and scratch database name - and per-lane setup and teardown commands that run inside the worktree; this is the gap that matters most for the owner's actual work, because two lanes each running a dev server collide on a port long before they collide in git. L3.4 replaces declared-only path claims with real detection using a three-way merge that touches no working tree, run before dispatch and again before queueing a merge, keeping declared claims as the cheap fast path."
    },
    {
      "id": "L4",
      "title": "The scheduler and the merge queue",
      "sessions": 4,
      "ownerGate": false,
      "kind": "deliver",
      "notes": "SPEC: docs/history/CONDUCTOR-KARVAN.md section L4. The highest-risk stage in the era, deliberately last before the surfaces. L4.1 adds a DAG scheduler behind a concurrency limit that DEFAULTS TO ONE, so no existing plan changes behaviour: the run loop is one sequential while and stage readiness picks the first ready stage, while the dependency graph is real and cycle-validated and its own doc comment admits execution stays sequential. Honour dependencies - worktree isolation cannot resolve a task dependency, and pretending otherwise is the most common failure in this whole tool category. L4.2 is the merge queue: serialize, rebase onto the current base, re-run the merge gate on the rebased tree, then integrate. Batch-then-bisect is the mature form and the stretch goal; serialize-and-rebase is correct on day one and it is what makes L1.3's branch-loss fix unnecessary rather than merely safe. L4.3 makes budget and tokens accounted per lane and capped globally, so the cost cap, the token cap and the approval flow still mean what they say when three sessions spend at once. L4.4 is the rehearsal and it is a deliverable, not a formality: a scratch two-repo plan run end to end with two lanes, proving both land, the verdict attributes each correctly, the ledger is one ledger, and neither lane's ports, database or build output touched the other's. Never rehearse against a real client repo."
    },
    {
      "id": "L5",
      "title": "The Face renders a fleet",
      "sessions": 3,
      "ownerGate": false,
      "kind": "deliver",
      "notes": "SPEC: docs/history/CONDUCTOR-KARVAN.md section L5. Every Face surface currently assumes exactly one live session. L5.1 renders N of them with the focused lane selectable. L5.2 shows per-lane cost, tokens, headroom and gate state while keeping the run-wide totals correct - it builds on the composable headroom widget the core plan's K4.4 shipped. L5.3 makes the kanban and the history surface lane-aware: which lane owns a card, which lane a commit came from. The golden tests are the regression net as always, and any baseline regenerates in a separate rebaseline commit."
    },
    {
      "id": "L6",
      "title": "Autonomy - the engine needs the human less",
      "sessions": 3,
      "ownerGate": false,
      "kind": "deliver",
      "notes": "SPEC: docs/history/CONDUCTOR-KARVAN.md section L6. Worth most once lanes exist, because N concurrent sessions is exactly when one human stops being able to watch. L6.1 brings the supervisor in-process: the watch verb is already the right event-driven shape and its own doc comment prices the polling alternative correctly, but the hook shells out to a model the engine does not own, does not budget, does not record in costs and cannot show in the Face; bring it in using the configured provider, give it its own cost line under the category that already exists, put its briefs in the store, surface it, and keep the external hook for people who want their own. L6.2 makes the advisor event-sourced - it currently prices itself at a flat rate per second, an estimate rather than a measurement, and re-reads a freshly composed context when the folded event projection is a better and far cheaper input; record its real token and cost rows. L6.3 is optional and off by default: one-way GitHub push only, a claim closing or commenting on a mapped issue and the run report posting as a PR comment. Nothing inbound, ever - the tracker is the verified contract and making it an eventually-consistent mirror of someone else's board is the failure this project's own anti-pattern list already names. An honest skip with a reason is an acceptable completion of L6.3."
    },
    {
      "id": "L7",
      "title": "Ship the lanes",
      "sessions": 2,
      "ownerGate": true,
      "kind": "deliver",
      "notes": "SPEC: docs/history/CONDUCTOR-KARVAN.md section L7. L7.1's acceptance is the owner's own two-repo job: a real plan across two of his repositories or two worktrees, run to completion, with the run record as the evidence - per-repo commits, per-lane cost, one ledger, no cross-talk. Docs and an ADR carry the concurrency model, the merge policy and the limits, and the architecture map gains the scheduler and the queue. L7.2 is the owner-signed merge, the tag, the release and the reinstall."
    }
  ],
  "gates": [
    {
      "name": "engine-fast",
      "command": "dotnet build Conductor.slnx -clp:ErrorsOnly",
      "optional": false,
      "tier": "fast",
      "parallel": false,
      "timeoutMinutes": 15
    },
    {
      "name": "face-fast",
      "command": "cmd /c \"cd /d C:\\code\\conductor\\face-go && go build ./... && go vet ./...\"",
      "optional": false,
      "tier": "fast",
      "parallel": false,
      "timeoutMinutes": 10
    },
    {
      "name": "engine-full",
      "command": "dotnet test Conductor.slnx",
      "optional": false,
      "tier": "full",
      "parallel": false,
      "timeoutMinutes": 40
    },
    {
      "name": "face-full",
      "command": "cmd /c \"cd /d C:\\code\\conductor\\face-go && go test ./...\"",
      "optional": false,
      "tier": "full",
      "parallel": false,
      "timeoutMinutes": 15
    }
  ],
  "gatePolicy": "perPhase",
  "verifyEachDelivery": true,
  "limits": {
    "stallMinutes": 15,
    "stallGraceMinutes": 3,
    "sessionTimeoutMinutes": 120,
    "batterySettleSeconds": 120,
    "authPreflight": true,
    "maxResumesPerSession": 2,
    "stageSlackFactor": 2,
    "backoffMinutes": 30,
    "maxBackoffs": 10,
    "maxRunCostUsd": 400,
    "approvalMode": false,
    "maxSessionTokens": 32000000,
    "softBreakRatio": 0.7,
    "maxConcurrentLanes": 2,
    "stallPatternTermination": true,
    "sameFailureCircuitBreaker": true,
    "stallBackoffMinutes": 12,
    "overheadCostPerSecond": 0.0001,
    "verifierThreshold": 80
  },
  "report": {
    "commit": false,
    "push": false,
    "heartbeatMinutes": 0
  },
  "telegram": {
    "allowedChatIds": [
      "99205495"
    ],
    "pollIntervalSeconds": 4,
    "enableTwoWay": true
  },
  "promptExtra": "TRAPS THIS REPO HAS ALREADY PAID FOR, in order of cost:\n0. THIS REPO'S .conductor/ IS THE LIVE STATE OF THE RUN DRIVING YOU. The claim and note verbs and the read-only verbs are yours; NEVER aim a run-control verb at this repo. Every live-run proof in this plan - and this plan is full of them - spawns YOUR build against a scratch repo with its own plan and its own state dir. You are building concurrency; a careless proof can corrupt the run judging you.\n1. NEVER REHEARSE AGAINST A REAL CLIENT REPO. The rehearsals in L4.4 and the acceptance in L7.1 are different things: L4.4 uses scratch repositories you create and destroy, L7.1 is the owner's real job and the owner decides when it runs. Creating a worktree in someone's working repository, or a branch in it, is not a proof - it is an incident.\n2. NEVER run tools/install.ps1 or overwrite the published engine. The conductor driving you runs from that published copy. The owner reinstalls at L7.2, not before.\n3. The conductor on PATH is the published engine driving you - NOT your working tree. Exercise YOUR changes through dotnet run --project src/Conductor -- VERB (or go run ./cmd/... in face-go). Exception by design: task claims go through the PATH copy, because they target the driving run.\n4. A SECOND CONDUCTOR RUN MAY SHARE THIS MACHINE. Never kill a conductor.exe, conductor-face.exe or stray dotnet process by pid: check its command line first and leave anything whose path is not this repo alone. Give every scratch rig its own port and its own state dir, and read the port back from the rig's own discovery file. This matters more in this plan than in any before it, because you are adding processes and worktrees on purpose.\n5. WORKTREES ARE NOT FREE AND THEY LEAK. Roughly half a gigabyte each in a repo this size, a practical ceiling around eight to ten concurrent, and the removal path currently swallows the IO exception a locked build output produces on Windows - so a failed cleanup leaves an orphan and no record. Every proof you write must clean up after itself, and if you cannot clean up, say so in the handoff with the path.\n6. A literal brace token in a file under this plan's templates directory KILLS THE ENGINE - the refusal goes to stderr only and fires when a stage first renders that template. Sweep after any template edit; a doubled brace emits a literal one.\n7. Keep the escalation token out of the tracker's handoff block unless you are escalating right now. The match is a plain substring, so prose describing the convention parks the run as hard as raising one, and the park then hot-loops and floods Telegram. The literal form is the word HUMAN followed by a colon.\n8. face-go: pad plain text then style, never width-format an ANSI string; clamp with MaxWidth and MaxHeight; goldens pin UTC - regenerate in a separate rebaseline commit, and read face-go/STYLE.md first.\n9. PowerShell tooling targets Windows PowerShell 5.1 and must stay ASCII-only. The ratchet gate exits 0 on a wrong path - a silent false green; never raise the pragma ceiling.\n10. Commit per checkpoint with the repo's trailer convention and push this plan's branch. Go sources are checked out LF; keep them that way.\n11. DEFAULT TO ONE. Every concurrency knob this plan adds ships with a default that leaves existing behaviour untouched. A plan that has to opt in cannot regress anybody, and that is the only way a change this deep is safe to release.",
  "templatesDir": "templates",
  "packs": [],
  "batteryCollapse": false,
  "readOrder": [
    "docs/history/CONDUCTOR-KARVAN.md"
  ],
  "analysisLanes": [],
  "workflows": {
    "deliver": {
      "name": "deliver",
      "description": "Deliver until the stage's checkpoints are confirmed; fix only when gates are red.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "deliver",
          "kind": "deliver",
          "deliver": true
        },
        {
          "id": "fix-if-red",
          "kind": "fix",
          "runIf": "!gatesGreen",
          "deliver": true
        }
      ],
      "repeat": true
    }
  },
  "defaultWorkflow": "deliver"
}
